# The Portfolio Meeting Nobody Read For

It's Monday, nine-oh-two. The chief development officer is at the head of the table with a mug that reads *board meeting fuel*. There's a printed portfolio list in front of her. Twenty names, six digits of asks, three columns of colored dots. Around her, seven development officers with their laptops half-open and their coffee still steaming. The intern is on the couch by the window taking notes nobody will ever read.

The meeting starts. Names are read out. Someone says *she's warm.* Someone else says *let me get back to you on that one.* A gift officer flips through a manila folder for a paragraph he knows he wrote in November about a lake house and cannot, right now, find. The meeting runs forty-five minutes and produces one action item, which is that someone will pull the wealth screen again before Thursday.

This is the most sacred hour in most development offices in the country.

It is also, we're going to gently say, a book club where nobody read the book.

Not because the officers didn't want to. Because the book was two hundred pages and nobody was given the weekend to read it.

## The ritual we borrowed and forgot to adapt

The portfolio meeting is a sales ritual dressed in nonprofit clothing. The bones are pure enterprise sales — pipeline review, stage transitions, forecasted close by quarter. It came into fundraising in the late nineties with the first wave of moves-management consultants, and it stuck, because it was the first thing that felt like a *system* in an office that had, for decades, run on personality and pluck.

That original system worked. In 1998, a portfolio meeting was the only structured moment in the week when the team pooled what they knew about the top hundred names. The knowledge was in scattered folders, in Rolodexes, in the assistant's memory. The meeting was the merge.

Twenty-eight years later, the knowledge is still scattered — it just lives in more expensive scattered places. A wealth-screen tab. A LinkedIn tab. A CRM screen with fourteen fields. A shared Google Doc from a strategy retreat in 2022. The merge no longer happens at the table, because nobody had time before Monday to open all six windows on the same name.

So the meeting persists, and everyone pretends. The dots get moved. The forecast gets updated. The intern takes notes. Nothing that happens in the room is stupider than what happens in most meetings anywhere. It is only that the room is *supposed* to be the smartest hour of the fundraising week — and it is not.

## Why we can't quit it

The meeting stays for the same reason the printed portfolio list stays. It is legible. It is defensible. It is the artifact the board asks about in the second quarter when the annual campaign is running behind.

Try telling a chief development officer that Monday's portfolio review is producing less useful information per hour than a fifteen-minute one-on-one. She will nod politely and keep the meeting on the calendar, because the meeting is not really for her. The meeting is for the finance committee, which needs to hear that *the pipeline is being actively managed.* The meeting is for the new gift officer, who needs a place to feel like part of a team. The meeting is for the board chair's Tuesday-morning question, which is *are we still on track?*

The meeting is theatre, and theatre is not stupid — it is, sometimes, load-bearing.

The problem isn't the room. The problem is that the room is the only place the reading happens, and the reading, in that room, is three minutes per name.

## What three minutes per name actually buys you

Three minutes gets you a wealth-screen tier and a last-gift date. It gets you a color of dot. It gets you a summary of the ask string.

It does not get you the November when the donor's daughter moved home. It does not get you the two paragraphs from the local business journal about the March sale. It does not get you the small line in her seventh-grade teacher's Facebook post that quietly explains, if you're paying attention, why the second-largest gift last spring was to a scholarship fund with her mother's name on it.

Three minutes is not a portrait. Three minutes is a mugshot with a price tag.

Every gift that has ever been made was a response to a portrait, not a mugshot. The officers around the table know this. They have been holding this contradiction in their heads for years — the gap between the meeting they are running and the fundraising they actually believe in — and if you ask them at a bar at the state association conference, they will tell you.

They don't quit because the alternative — actually reading, for ninety minutes per name, before Monday — was not, until recently, an option a human being could sustain across a hundred-name portfolio.

## The ninety-minute portrait, restored

Here is the small structural change that turns the room back into what it was supposed to be.

Not a new meeting. Not a new dashboard. Not a new tier. A change in what is on the printed page in front of each officer when she sits down at nine-oh-two on Monday.

Instead of twenty names with three columns of colored dots, imagine each officer walking in with three portraits. Two pages each. Sourced. Current to Sunday night. The March liquidity event is there. The daughter's move-home is there. The Facebook post from the seventh-grade teacher is quoted, with a link. The recommended ask is there, with the reasoning visible. The *do not bring up the ex-husband* is there. So is the *this is her first spring since her mother passed — pause the appeal cadence one more quarter.*

Now the meeting is different. The officer says *I read her. Here is what I saw. Here is what I want to do.* The colleague across the table says *here is what I saw last October that isn't in the portrait.* The chief nods, decides, moves on. The meeting takes twenty minutes instead of forty-five. The three-minute-per-name pretense is gone, because the reading has already happened — and the room can spend its hour on the *decision*, which is the thing the room was invented for in the first place.

The dot-moving becomes a five-minute software chore anyone can do on Thursday. The reading becomes what a machine does over the weekend, so Monday's officers can do what only officers can do — read the portrait, choose the move, and pick up the phone.

That is where Rōmy sits. Not in the meeting. In the folder each officer opens the night before.

## What we want for Monday

We don't want the portfolio meeting to die. We want it to feel like the smartest hour of your week again.

We want the officer who used to fumble for the November note to walk in with the November note quoted on page one. We want the intern on the couch to take notes on decisions, not on the search for information the team should have had before it sat down. We want the finance committee's Tuesday-morning question to be answered with *we made three specific moves this week, here they are* — instead of *the pipeline is being actively managed.*

We want the meeting to be short, and the reading to be long, and the reading to happen somewhere other than the meeting.

We want you to have your Sunday nights back too, but that is a different post.

## A small assignment, with love

Next Monday, before your portfolio meeting, pick one name from the list. The name you are most afraid to bring up because you know the least about her.

Spend twenty quiet minutes before the meeting reading everything you can find on her. Not in the CRM. In the local newspaper. In the obituary section. In the annual report of the peer nonprofit down the road. In her town's chamber of commerce newsletter. In her college's alumni magazine from the year she chaired the reunion committee.

Walk into the meeting and, when her row comes up, do not say *she's warm.* Say the two sentences you would have said if you had done ninety minutes of reading, not twenty. The rest of the room will feel the difference. Somebody will ask how you did it. You will say, quietly, *I read her.*

Then, on the drive home, notice that the twenty minutes was, this morning, the best twenty minutes of the meeting.

Now imagine what happens when it isn't your twenty. When it's a machine's ninety, done Sunday night while you were on the porch with your kids, and the whole room walks in Monday morning with the portrait already read.

That's the meeting we're trying, quietly, to give you back. ♡
